Assessment and mitigation of light pollution
Botao Liu*, Huiru Yang, Jia Li
School of Economics and Management, Beijing Jiaotong University, 264401
*Corresponding author: 22711052@bjtu.edu.cn
Abstract. With the improvement of human development level, excessive optical radiation has caused adverse effects on human living and production environment, namely, light pollution. In this paper, a system for assessing the degree of light pollution is studied and methods for more effective intervention of light pollution are proposed. On the basis of collecting data related to light pollution, we first preprocessed and extracted information related to 14 indicators, including brightness, light range, population and distance from the sea, from five dimensions of light intensity, social development, human activities, biodiversity and geographical climate, so as to quantitatively analyze the degree of light pollution. Then combined with the analytic hierarchy process (AHP) and entropy weight method (EWM) to calculate the weight of each index, and at last, the sensitivity analysis was used to verify its robustness. Among them, the building distribution density, brightness and distance from the sea have the greatest weight. At the same time, K-means clustering algorithm was used to calculate the standard, and finally an evaluation system was calculated to measure the severity of light pollution.
Keywords: AHP, EWM, K-means, GM (1,1), TSF, CPM.
1. Introduction
With more than half of the world's population living in urbanized areas, light pollution is a growing problem, and the current increase in light pollution is rapid and widespread. Outdoor lighting is considered necessary for production in modern society. However, the widespread use of artificial light has greatly changed the natural pattern of darkness, affected people's circadian rhythm, and caused adverse effects on people's body, such as insomnia, depression and other problems; Artificial light can also interfere with migration and animal reproduction, and unnecessary light pollution can also contribute to air pollution. In the view of those problems, we can reduce the impact of light pollution by adopting intervention strategies such as reducing the intensity and duration of light exposure, thus reducing the risk level of urban or rural areas. In addition, through these intervention strategies, a balance can be struck between the actual needs of social development and green and low-carbon development.
1.1. Restatement of the Problem
In this problem, it is very important to propose an effective method to evaluate the degree of light pollution in an area. First of all, we need an index system to evaluate the degree of light pollution. Four different types of regions are evaluated through the index system. Then we have to propose feasible intervention strategies to curb light pollution and use indicators to calculate the most effective intervention strategies. Finally, we publicize effective intervention strategies for light pollution in an area through posters.
